# ESP-IDF C++ development

ESP-IDF is a C SDK with a C++ toolchain (default `-std=gnu++26` on chip). Write application code in C++. Keep `main/` thin: `extern "C" app_main` plus wiring. Put reusable logic in `components/<name>/`. Do not enable exceptions or RTTI unless the user explicitly asks. This tree currently has both off.

Unit tests: follow `esp-idf-cpp-unit-tests` instead of this file.

## Components and CMake

```
components/<name>/
  include/<name>/   # public headers only
  src/              # .cpp and private headers
  CMakeLists.txt
```

List `SRCS` explicitly (no `GLOB` / `SRC_DIRS` in production). Public headers go in `INCLUDE_DIRS`; private ones stay next to sources (`PRIV_INCLUDE_DIRS` if needed).

- `REQUIRES`: headers included from **public** headers, plus anything dependents must link.
- `PRIV_REQUIRES`: headers included only from `.cpp` / private headers (`freertos`, `esp_timer`, drivers).
- Do not put FreeRTOS or driver types in public headers if a `void*` / opaque handle will do (keeps headers mockable and C++-only consumers simpler).
- Managed deps: `idf.py add-dependency` → `idf_component.yml`. `espressif/esp-idf-cxx` is RAII wrappers for GPIO/I2C/SPI/timer/events; it expects exceptions. Do not add it while exceptions stay disabled.

Override the language standard only per-component, after `idf_component_register`:

```cmake
target_compile_options(${COMPONENT_LIB} PRIVATE -std=gnu++26)
```

Use `PUBLIC` only if public headers need that standard.

## C / C++ boundary

`app_main` must have C linkage. Same for any symbol called from C.

```cpp
extern "C" void app_main(void)
{
    static app::Thing thing;
    thing.start();
}
```

Headers used from both languages:

```cpp
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
void my_c_api(void);
#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
```

Define the C++ side as `extern "C" void my_c_api(void) { ... }`.

## Language subset (this project)

- **No exceptions.** Do not `throw`, `try`/`catch`, or throw from constructors. Return `bool` / `esp_err_t`. Log failures with `ESP_LOGx` and `esp_err_to_name(err)`.
- **No RTTI.** Do not use `dynamic_cast` or `typeid`. Prefer static dispatch, templates, or a known closed set of types. Virtuals at a component boundary (like `platform::RuntimeComponent`) are fine; vtables live in flash.
- **No `iostream`.** Including `<iostream>` / `<sstream>` adds ~200 kB. Use `ESP_LOG`, `snprintf`, or `esp_log`.
- **No `setjmp`/`longjmp`.** They skip destructors.
- Do not enable `CONFIG_COMPILER_CXX_EXCEPTIONS` or `CONFIG_COMPILER_CXX_RTTI` in `sdkconfig` / `sdkconfig.defaults` to “make STL happier.” They grow flash (RTTI: tens of kB) and exception unwind is not real-time safe.

## Naming

Use descriptive names for classes, methods, and variables. A reader should know the role without comments or the surrounding function. Prefer a longer clear name over a short vague one (`drop_count_`, `post_event_from_isr`, `Mailbox` — not `dc`, `pefi`, `MB`).

- **Classes / enums / structs:** PascalCase nouns (`Mailbox`, `MessageType`). Name what the type *is*, not a generic `Manager` / `Helper` / `Data`.
- **Methods:** snake_case verbs that say what happens and from where (`receive`, `post_from_isr`, `uses_reserved_slots`). Pair task-vs-ISR APIs with an explicit `_from_isr` suffix. Booleans read as predicates (`started_`, `has_message`).
- **Variables / members:** snake_case; members end with `_` (`items_available_`, `control_task_`). Name the meaning, not the type (`timeout_ms` not `t` or `uint32_val`). Loop indices `i`/`n` are fine in a few lines; anything that lives longer is not.
- **Constants:** `k` prefix + PascalCase (`kCapacity`, `kControlStackBytes`, `kTag`).
- **Namespaces:** match the component (`platform`, `demo`).
- Abbreviations only when they are IDF or domain standard (`isr`, `gpio`, `nvs`, `wdt`). Do not invent `cfg`, `ptr`, `tmp`, `obj`, `buf` unless the buffer’s contents are obvious in the next line.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD
void p(Msg& m, uint32_t t);
class MB { int dc; void* q; };

// ✅ GOOD
bool post(const Message& message, uint32_t timeout_ms);
class Mailbox {
    uint32_t drop_count_{0};
    void* items_available_{nullptr};
};
```

## Types, headers, RAII

Namespaces match the component (`platform`, `demo`). `#pragma once`. Include public headers as `"platform/mailbox.hpp"`.

- Rule of Five: if you define a destructor (e.g. delete a semaphore), `= delete` copy. Add move only if ownership transfer is real.
- Constructors must not fail by throwing. Failed RTOS object creation: log, leave handles null, make later calls safe no-ops, or abort on unrecoverable boot failures.
- Prefer bounded storage (`std::array`, member buffers, `static constexpr` capacities) over `std::vector` / `new[]` on hot paths. Heap fragmentation is a firmware bug.
- STL that is fine: `std::array`, `std::span` (if available), `std::optional` for values, scoped enums, `constexpr`, `nullptr`. Avoid `std::function` in ISRs (heap + type erasure). Avoid `<filesystem>` unless a VFS is actually in use (many operations unimplemented: links, `space`, `resize_file`, `current_path` is always `/`).

```cpp
// ❌ BAD: exception + iostream + heap on the control path
void start() {
    std::cout << "hi\n";
    auto* q = new std::vector<int>();
    throw std::runtime_error("fail");
}

// ✅ GOOD: C API, bounded state, explicit error
esp_err_t start() {
    if (handle_ == nullptr) {
        ESP_LOGE(kTag, "init failed");
        return ESP_FAIL;
    }
    return ESP_OK;
}
```

## Designated initializers

C++26 (IDF default) is stricter than C. When filling IDF config structs:

- Members in **declaration order**.
- No nested designated init, no mixing designated + positional, no designated array init.

```cpp
gpio_config_t io = {
    .pin_bit_mask = 1ULL << pin,
    .mode = GPIO_MODE_OUTPUT,
    .pull_up_en = GPIO_PULLUP_DISABLE,
    .pull_down_en = GPIO_PULLDOWN_DISABLE,
    .intr_type = GPIO_INTR_DISABLE,
};
```

Zero the rest first if you only set a few fields: `gpio_config_t io{}; io.mode = GPIO_MODE_OUTPUT;`.

## Tasks and threading

Prefer the FreeRTOS C API (this repo’s pattern): `xTaskCreate`, static **thunk** + instance method, explicit stack size and priority.

```cpp
static void control_task_thunk(void* arg)
{
    static_cast<Runtime*>(arg)->control_loop();
}
```

`std::thread` / `std::mutex` / `std::condition_variable` sit on pthreads → FreeRTOS. Default pthread stacks are often too small. If you use them, set `esp_pthread_cfg_t` (`esp_pthread_set_cfg`) for stack, name, priority, core. Destroy `std::jthread` only from a pthread/C++-thread task, not from a raw `xTaskCreate` task.

Do not block the scheduler or hold a lock across long work. Feed the task WDT in long loops (`esp_task_wdt_reset`). Prefer static or ctor-created RTOS objects over create/delete in a hot loop.

## ISRs and IRAM

Keep ISRs short: clear the source, `FromISR` give/queue, set `*yielded` / `portYIELD_FROM_ISR`. No logs, no locks that block, no heap, no virtual calls.

Vtables and most code live in flash. With cache disabled (flash write/erase), virtual dispatch in an `ESP_INTR_FLAG_IRAM` handler crashes. `IRAM_ATTR` on a lambda is not reliable (GCC ignores section attributes on lambdas; callees may still be in flash).

```cpp
static void IRAM_ATTR interrupt_handler_static(void* arg)
{
    static_cast<Driver*>(arg)->interrupt_handler();
}

void IRAM_ATTR Driver::interrupt_handler()
{
    bool yielded = false;
    runtime.post_event_from_isr(event, &yielded);
    if (yielded) {
        portYIELD_FROM_ISR();
    }
}
```

Define IRAM handlers in a `.cpp`, not inline in the class body. The linker script generator cannot place **C++-linkage** functions at function granularity; `IRAM_ATTR` / `extern "C"` wrappers are the supported placement method.

## Logging and size

- Tag: `constexpr char kTag[] = "mailbox";` in an anonymous namespace in the `.cpp`.
- `ESP_LOGI` / `W` / `E`; format with `PRIu32` etc. from `<inttypes.h>`.
- Do not pull unused IDF components. Measure with `idf.py size` / `size-components` / `size-files` before “optimizing.”
